jt-pve-storage-dellemc (0.7.63~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * BETA RELEASE. Not for production use.
  * POWERVAULT: the plugin could not find the host it had just created.
    Reported from an ME4024. Every activation created the host, failed to
    find it, asked again, and the array refused with "The specified host
    name is already in use. (return code -10389)"; the storage went inactive
    and stayed there. 'show host-groups' answers with a TREE - groups at the
    top, hosts nested inside them - and this plugin read a top-level 'hosts'
    array that is not there. The fallback then returned the host GROUPS,
    whose names are group names, so no lookup could ever match. The answer
    is now walked rather than indexed, a group is never handed back as a
    host, and a name differing only in case resolves as the array's own
    uniqueness check would resolve it.
  * POWERVAULT: a second line of defence for the same failure, since another
    firmware may answer in a shape nobody has seen. The array's own -10389
    is now taken as proof the host exists - read from the return code, never
    from the wording - and the question that matters for data safety, are
    THIS node's initiators on that host, is settled by asking the array to
    attach them rather than by assuming. If that cannot be established the
    storage still refuses: mapping a volume to a host that might belong to
    another node is how two nodes come to write to one disk.
  * POWERVAULT: every pool reported itself 100% full, so PVE would refuse
    every allocation. Free space was read as 'avail', the column heading
    'show pools' PRINTS; the pools basetype documents 'total-avail', and the
    ME4024 carries no field by the printed name. A field the array does not
    have reads as zero, which is indistinguishable from a full pool.
  * FC: "No FC target ports are visible from this node" on a healthy fabric,
    on every 'pvesm status'. The kernel names a remote port
    rport-<host>:<channel>-<remote>, with a COLON after the host number. The
    scan asked for three hyphen-separated numbers, which matches no entry
    the kernel has ever created, so the target list was always empty. The
    warning now goes through the once-an-hour path instead of a bare warn on
    a ten-second poll, and says what was seen rather than only what was not.
  * _cmd gained allow_codes: an expected refusal is recognised by the
    array's own return code rather than by its wording.

 -- Jason Cheng <jason@jason.tools>  Tue, 04 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-dellemc (0.7.62~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * BETA RELEASE. Not for production use.
  * FIRST DEFECT FOUND ON REAL HARDWARE. A PowerVault ME4024 could not be
    added at all: `pvesm add` failed with "GET /show/system returned a body
    that is not JSON: Wide character in subroutine entry". JSON expects
    bytes, and HTTP::Response::decoded_content returns characters - it
    decodes by the Content-Type charset, and for any text/* without one it
    falls back to ISO-8859-1, so every byte above 0x7F becomes a wide
    character. The ME CLI answers text/*, and this array's /show/system
    carried one non-ASCII character. Every body is read as bytes now, in all
    three families.
  * A body that is not JSON is quoted in the error, up to 200 printable
    characters. On a first hardware run the difference between an HTML error
    page, an empty body and a CLI banner is the whole diagnosis, and "not
    JSON" alone gives none of it.

