jt-pve-storage-dellemc (0.7.4~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * BETA RELEASE. Not for production use. No storage type has been run
    against physical hardware.
  * The storage API version is negotiated instead of hardcoded. PVE 9 raised
    APIVER twice inside the 9.1 point releases; claiming more than the running
    PVE makes it reject the plugin and every storage of these types disappears
    from the node, and claiming less makes PVE warn on every load of
    PVE::Storage, which is once per pvesm call.
  * volume_resize now knows about the $snapname parameter added in storage API
    14 and refuses it, rather than silently resizing the volume the snapshot
    was taken from.
  * Deleting a snapshot now first removes the temporary clone that was reading
    it. vzdump in snapshot mode takes a snapshot, reads it through path(), and
    deletes it the moment the backup finishes; an array will not delete a
    snapshot something was cloned from, so every such backup would have failed
    at cleanup and left the clone behind on the array and its device on the
    host.
  * Rolling back to anything other than the most recent snapshot is refused.
    Dell documents what a restore does to the volume and says nothing about
    snapshots taken after the restore point; on an array that discards them
    PVE would keep listing restore points that no longer exist. New option
    dell-rollback-any-snapshot lifts it once the behaviour is verified.
  * The orphan reaper leaves alone any device that still has a working
    multipath path. A disk hot-added to a running VM is briefly absent from
    the array's listing while the guest already has it open, and a guest's
    open file descriptor is not a holder or a mount.
  * An outage is now declared on elapsed time rather than on a count of
    consecutive failed polls, and activate_storage records the failure it
    dies on. PVE stops polling a storage it has marked inactive, so a counter
    that waits for three failures would stay silent through exactly the
    outages that matter, and activate_storage dying means status() is never
    reached at all.
  * volume_snapshot_info and rename_snapshot are implemented rather than
    inherited: the base versions read a qcow2 file through filesystem_path,
    which this plugin cannot provide.
  * PowerVault answers the confirmation prompt on rollback, as the CLI
    Reference requires of a script.
  * PowerFlex reports real snapshot timestamps instead of 1970.
  * pve-dell-config-get bounds its mount and umount. It runs during an outage,
    against storage that may be half dead.

 -- Jason Cheng <jason@jason.tools>  Mon, 27 Jul 2026 03:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-dellemc (0.7.3~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * BETA RELEASE. Not for production use. No storage type has been run
    against physical hardware.
  * Cross-checked against the production incident records of the two related
    projects (jt-pve-storage-netapp, jt-pve-storage-purestorage). Nine issues
    of the same classes were present here and are fixed.
  * A delete the array refused could be reported to PVE as success, because
    $@ was read after intervening evals had reset it. PVE would then drop the
    disk from the VM configuration while the volume was still on the array.
  * free_image no longer proceeds to delete when the array fails to say which
    hosts a volume is mapped to. Deleting a mapped volume leaves every node it
    is mapped to with a device that answers nothing.
  * The orphan reaper no longer falls back to a per-volume array query when a
    listing carries no WWID. That was one call per volume on every poll on
    every node. A listing that returns no WWIDs at all now abandons the pass
    rather than treating every volume as deleted.
  * Temporary clones created to read a snapshot are recorded per node and
    removed when the process that created them is gone. They have no PVE
    volume name, so nothing else would ever find them.
  * PowerStore collection paging follows the Content-Range the array returns
    instead of assuming a short page means the end. A server-side page cap
    would otherwise hide volumes and make the reaper treat live ones as
    deleted.
  * PowerVault and PowerFlex wait for an object to become visible after
    creating it, as PowerStore already did.
  * PowerFlex unmaps before deleting in every rollback path, and rolls back a
    clone this node cannot map.
  * The block-device test that follows a device glob now runs inside the same
    timeout as the glob.
  * New test t/11-imports.t: perl -c compiles a call to an undefined
    subroutine without complaint, so a helper used without its use line only
    fails at runtime. One such slip was found this way.

